<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Genet Sel Evol</journal-id><journal-title>Genetics, Selection, Evolution : GSE</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0999-193X</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1297-9686</issn><publisher><publisher-name>BioMed Central</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">16790227</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2689290</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">1297-9686-38-4-359</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/1297-9686-38-4-359</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Variance components for survival of piglets at farrowing using a reduced animal model</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib id="A1" corresp="yes" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>White</surname><given-names>Ian MS</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1">1</xref><email>i.m.s.white@ed.ac.uk</email></contrib><contrib id="A2" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Roehe</surname><given-names>Rainer</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I2">2</xref></contrib><contrib id="A3" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Knap</surname><given-names>Pieter W</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I3">3</xref></contrib><contrib id="A4" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Brotherstone</surname><given-names>Sue</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1">1</xref><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I2">2</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="I1"><label>1</label>Institute of Evolutionary Biology, University of Edinburgh, Ashworth Laboratories, West Mains Road, Edinburgh EH9 3JT, UK</aff><aff id="I2"><label>2</label>Sustainable Livestock Systems Group, Scottish Agriculture College, Bush Estate, Penicuik, Midlothian EH26 0PH, UK</aff><aff id="I3"><label>3</label>PIC International Group, Ratsteich 31, D-24837 Schleswig, Germany</aff><pub-date pub-type="collection"><year>2006</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>23</day><month>6</month><year>2006</year></pub-date><volume>38</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>359</fpage><lpage>370</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.gsejournal.org/content/38/4/359"/><history><date date-type="received"><day>16</day><month>8</month><year>2005</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>20</day><month>2</month><year>2006</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2006 INRA, EDP Sciences</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2006</copyright-year><copyright-holder>INRA, EDP Sciences</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>Farrowing survival is usually analysed as a trait of the sow, but this precludes estimation of any direct genetic effects associated with individual piglets. In order to estimate these effects, which are particularly important for sire lines, it is necessary to fit an animal model. However this can be computationally very demanding. We show how direct and maternal genetic effects can be estimated with a simpler analysis based on the reduced animal model and we illustrate the method using farrowing survival information on 118 193 piglets in 10 314 litters. We achieve a 30% reduction in computing time and a 70% reduction in memory use, with no important loss of accuracy. This use of the reduced animal model is not only of interest for pig breeding but also for poultry and fish breeding where large full-sib families are performance tested.</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>reduced animal model</kwd><kwd>piglet survival</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front><body><p>"To access the full article, please see PDF"</p></body></article>